GLOSSARY

"Anthropic's templates" - It refers to Anthropic's open-source example repository anthropics/financial-services, which shows a recommended way to structure role-based agents as plugins. This project copies that structure. When the write-up says "modeled on Anthropic's pattern," this repo is what it means

Vertical plugin — the shared plugin ("Digital Asset Risk"). Holds the skills, the slash commands, and the Metrika connector that all three agents rely on. Installed first; it's the single source of truth.

Agent plugin — a plugin for one agent (Risk Controller, Onboarding DD, or Risk Reporting). Contains that agent's system prompt plus its own copies of the skills it needs, so it's self-contained.

Claude Cowork ("Cowork") — a mode of Claude built for getting work done, where agents, skills, and connected tools run. These plugins are used in Cowork (in plain "Chat" mode, the agent parts are inactive).

Claude Code — Anthropic's command-line coding tool. It uses the same plugin format, which is why the install steps mention it as an alternative.

Connector — a link from Claude to an outside service so it can read that service's data. Here, the connector links to Metrika.

Skill — a self-contained "how we do X" reference that Claude loads automatically when relevant (a SKILL.md file). Example: the morning-brief skill tells the agent how to build a briefing. Skills are plain text — editing one needs no coding.

Slash command — a shortcut you type starting with / (e.g. /morning-brief) that kicks off a defined task. A faster alternative to describing the task in words.

Plugin — a bundle that packages skills, commands, connectors, and sub-agents so you get a ready-to-use setup in one install. This project ships four plugins (one shared + three agents).

Guardrail floor — the three skills every agent must include (review-gate, risk-evidence, metrika-mcp). A check script fails the build if any agent drops them.

Agent — in the Claude sense, a configured "persona + instructions + tools" that carries out a kind of task. Each of the three deliverables is an agent.

Sub-agent — an agent bundled inside a plugin that Claude can call on. These only run in Cowork.

System prompt — the built-in instructions that define an agent's role, what it produces, and its rules. In this project it's the agents/<name>.md file for each agent.

Mutation gate — the rule that before any mutation, the agent must stop, explain what it would do, and wait for an explicit human "yes" — and even then only ever create a draft.

Draft work product — the core principle: every agent output is a draft for a qualified human to review and sign off, never a final decision or an executed action.

Read-only vs mutation tools — read-only tools just look at Metrika data (safe; the default). Mutation tools change something in Metrika and cost money to run. The agents avoid mutations by default.

